    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d489">

                <pb facs="0672" n="662" xml:id="pg662"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20/11–2345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Robertson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-11-23T15:00:00+07:00">November 23,
                            1945—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received, November 23—8:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2023. General Wedemeyer has sent long top secret message<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d489-f1"/> to General George C. Marshall<anchor n="61"
                        xml:id="d489-f2"/> summarizing military situation in China and making
                    certain recommendations which require political as well as military
                    consideration at highest policy levels. Message was discussed in meeting in
                    General Wedemeyer’s apartment in Shanghai Monday evening November 19 attended by
                    Lieutenant General Stratemeyer, Commanding General, U. S. Army Air Forces, China
                    Theater; Major General Maddocks, Chief of Staff, China Theater; Major General
                    McClure, Commanding General, Chinese Combat Command; Colonel Creasy, Commanding
                    Officer, Residual Teams, Kunming; and myself. 1 was asked if I agreed with
                    conclusions reached. I replied that if premises as to military situation were
                    correct (which of course were not subject to question by me) I heartily
                    concurred. No copies of message were made available to me and I was asked to
                    hold information contained therein highest confidence. I assume message will be
                    immediately discussed with the Secretary and Ambassador Hurley.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Robertson</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d489-f1" n="60" xml:id="d489-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram CFBX 15120, November 20, <ref target="#pg650">p. 650</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d489-f2" n="61" xml:id="d489-f2-content">
                <p>General of the Army Marshall had been succeeded as Chief of Staff by General of
                    the Army Dwight D. Eisenhower.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
